{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791305165,"nonce":"4MOH2u0p464M7dLdA3jPbe6zOZA=","title":"Lua: os.clock reports elapsed wall time instead of process CPU time","message":"crates/zyntax_lua/src/host.rs host_clock uses a OnceLock\u003cInstant\u003e and returns elapsed(), although its contract comment and Lua os.clock describe CPU time. Time spent sleeping or waiting for first-call compilation is therefore included, while Lua/LuaJIT os.clock excludes sleep. Conversely, process CPU consumed by background compiler threads is not represented as CPU time.\n\nThe Lua benchmark scripts time kernels with os.clock, so cross-runtime kernel columns use different clocks today; before/after comparisons within zylua and externally measured process wall time remain like-for-like. Use a separate standard elapsed-time timer for kernel comparisons, and implement the language API with process CPU time. Verify with a program that samples os.clock around a deliberate sleep, plus CPU work. Do not change the clock and call the resulting smaller timings a compiler speedup.","files":null}]}